00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.869 align:start position:0%
the chaos at kabul airport last august  symbolized for many the pain and failure  of the 20-year war by the us and its  allies  with the sudden collapse of the afghan  government crowds stormed the airport  trying to get out  some desperately hung onto the fuselages  of departing planes only to fall to  their deaths  twenty-year-old market stall worker  suleiman safi was one of them  he told his mother he wanted to earn  more money to support the family and pay  for his younger brother's medical  treatment  he said to me mother they are taking  people abroad for free and if you allow  me i will go somewhere and make money  for my brother's treatment  i said no this is a lie don't do this he  left anyway  thousands more people besieged the  outside of the airport held back by  taliban fighters  hayatuddin hyatt worked for american  contractors as a security guard and  wanted to be flown out  i went to the airport twice with my kids  but couldn't get through the crowds to  the gates so i came back home  for many hopes of getting out faded with  the departure of the last military  transport late on the night of august  the 30th  it's the helmets and the body armor an  event marked by thunderous taliban  gunfire into the skies that began as we  were on air  since then with international support  and technical help airport operations  have been restored with the resumption  of some chartered and scheduled flights  even when flights out of afghanistan are  back to normal hurdles will remain for  people wanting to take them there are  problems getting passports and visas  then finding a country willing to take  them in  many like hayet are still waiting for a  special visa that will entitle him to  settle with his family in the u.s  there's no work here and my kids have no  future my daughters used to go to school  but now they sit at home  three months after the end of the u.s  presence here the sudden departure and  the trauma caused by it will likely be  felt for months if not years to come  rob mcbride al jazeera herrat  afghanistan
 

